      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "b9e8cf2952fbef1569d889bcc901c480",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">(Ibis numbers rows from 0.) What can be proved about these columns?\nLean knows that <code>LAG(ts)</code> comes from an <strong>earlier</strong> row of the same\npartition, so <code>prev_ts &lt;= ts</code>; that a lagged partition column equals the\nrow's own; and that <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> is never negative. Guard the first\nrow's NULL with <code>isnull() | \u2026</code>, as in the contract below.</span>\n<h2 id=\"ties-when-previous-isnt-well-defined\">Ties: when \"previous\" isn't well defined</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Sensors sometimes report twice in the same instant. If two readings of\na sensor have the <strong>same</strong> <code>ts</code>, which one is \"previous\"? SQL doesn't\nsay; the engine picks, and may pick differently on the next run. A\nproof about one choice would say nothing about the other. So unless\n<code>requires</code> gives a key on the partition and order columns, Lean\nreports every postcondition as <em>undetermined</em> and\n<code>report.deterministic</code> is <code>False</code>. With the key, earlier means strictly\nearlier: <code>prev_ts &lt; ts</code>.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
