    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "71973a19773fad923745cb460ddc4e8e",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">The theorem that makes the keyed case trustworthy is <code>window_tiebreak</code>\n(<code>SubstraitLean/Keys.lean</code>): when a window's partition and sort columns\ncontain a key of its input, <strong>every</strong> way of sorting the input by the\nwindow's keys gives the same rows. So the proof describes what\nDataFusion computes, however it breaks ties internally.</span>\n<h2 id=\"asof-joins-aligning-two-sensors\">ASOF joins: aligning two sensors</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">An accelerometer samples at 100 Hz, a gyroscope at 130 Hz, so their\ntimestamps rarely line up. To train a model on both you want, for each\naccelerometer sample, the <strong>latest gyroscope sample at or before it</strong>,\nand only if it is recent enough. That is an <em>ASOF join</em>. DataFusion\ndoesn't have one (and Ibis's <code>asof_join</code> compiles to DuckDB syntax), so\n<code>sv.asof_join</code> builds it from parts Lean understands: a range join, then\n<code>ROW_NUMBER</code> to keep the latest match.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The model will be trained on these pairs, so a gyroscope reading from\nthe future would quietly leak information it can't have in production.\nThe contract says what an ASOF join promises: one output row per\naccelerometer sample, never a gyroscope sample from the future, and\nnever one older than the tolerance.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
